    "description": "Iran's cleaning-products industry includes laundry powders, dishwashing liquids, soaps and other household formulations. Historical research traces its roots in soap manufacture, while trade documentation and company catalogues show its overlap with personal-care manufacturing. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Detergents", "Household products", "Soap", "Consumer goods", "Chemical manufacturing"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:01:35Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:01:35Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Detergents and Cleaning Products in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover the historical roots and product diversity of Iran's cleaning-products industry, from soap manufacture to household and personal-care brands."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "product-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Several industries meet on the household shelf",
        "content": "Iran's detergent sector is best understood through distinct product groups. The Trade Promotion Organization of Iran's directory lists laundry powders, dishwashing liquids, washing liquids and soaps, alongside bleach and personal-care products. Its company entries show that manufacturers may participate in several of these categories. That commercial overlap does not make the products identical or justify combining all their output into a single detergent statistic. [s2]\n\nThe directory also identifies specialized dishwasher products, including tablets, rinse aids and regenerating salt. Its breadth is useful evidence of product differentiation. As an undated directory, however, it cannot establish which listed factories remain operational, which formulations are currently available or how much each producer sells. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-roots",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Soap making before packaged detergents",
        "content": "Historian Willem Floor's account in Encyclopaedia Iranica documents a long history of soap manufacture in Persia. Production was dispersed among towns and workshops rather than beginning with modern consumer-goods factories. In the Qajar period, towns and villages maintained local soap-making traditions. [s1]\n\nThe account describes growing interest in imported, perfumed and attractively wrapped soap, followed by attempts to modernize domestic manufacture. A toilet-soap plant established in Tehran did not immediately displace traditional workshops. Later industrial development expanded the product landscape to synthetic detergents and other cleaning preparations. The historical lesson is gradual diversification: older manufacturing practices and newer factory products coexisted, rather than changing in a single nationwide transition. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "brands-and-boundaries",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Household cleaning and personal care",
        "content": "Pakrokh's English-language website provides one company example of the industry's broader hygiene setting. It traces the business to Dr. Hamidi Laboratory in 1963 and names brands including Latifeh, Iren, Silvergate and Korend. Its product navigation includes shampoos and hair conditioners. These details document how the company presents its history and range; its claims about market standing are not independent measurements. [s3]\n\nThis example helps explain a recurring classification problem. A manufacturer described broadly as a hygiene-products business may sell products used on clothing, household surfaces, hair or skin. Research must identify the particular product before assigning production, sales or trade figures to detergents. A brand list can establish examples of commercial activity, but cannot by itself establish national market size. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evidence-and-relevance",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Understanding the sector beyond brand familiarity",
        "content": "The inspected sources connect a familiar consumer category with a longer manufacturing history. Iranica supplies historical context; the trade directory records a differentiated industrial product range; and a company website illustrates branded personal-care activity. Together they support a qualitative account, with different limits for each kind of evidence. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nA current industrial assessment still requires comparable production and trade records by product. It also needs independent evidence before making claims about cleaning performance, environmental advantages or market leadership. The available material supports describing the sector's diversity, while leaving its current scale and the performance of individual products unresolved. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
