    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iranian manufacturers document a range of coating products and resin families, including alkyd, acrylic and unsaturated polyester materials. Company portfolios show connections among paint manufacture, automotive repair, composites and other downstream industries, although they do not establish national production totals. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Paints", "Resins", "Coatings", "Polymers", "Industrial materials", "Manufacturing"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:01:35Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:01:35Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Paints, Coatings and Resins in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore documented Iranian paint and resin manufacturing, its regional examples and connections with coatings, automotive repair and composites."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "sector-scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Related products with different industrial roles",
        "content": "Iran's paints and resins sector includes companies supplying finished coatings and companies supplying materials to downstream manufacturers. Rakhshan Resin Tehran, for example, describes operations spanning industrial paints, alkyd resins and unsaturated polyester resins. Simab Resin presents a different emphasis: water-based acrylic resins and polyurethane resins supplied across several industries. [s1][s2]\n\nThese portfolios show why the sector cannot be understood simply as the manufacture of wall paint. Resin suppliers can serve markets outside coatings, while a coatings business may produce several chemically distinct product families. Company catalogues help identify that breadth, but do not reveal the national balance between the different activities. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "documented-producers",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Manufacturing examples across industrial regions",
        "content": "Rakhshan Resin Tehran says it began activities in 2000 and obtained an operating licence for production in Eshtehard Industrial Park, in Alborz province. It describes subsequent expansion of its product range to include orthophthalic and isophthalic unsaturated polyester resins. Its catalogue also distinguishes alkyd, epoxy and polyurethane coating categories. These are manufacturer-reported details rather than an independent plant audit. [s1]\n\nSimab Resin places its factory in Kaveh Industrial City near Saveh and dates the start of operations to 1991. Petro Octane Isatis identifies factories in Yazd province and lists polyester and vinylester resins, alkyd resins, putties and paint driers. Together these examples demonstrate activity beyond a single manufacturing location, without establishing a complete national map or ranking industrial clusters. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "downstream-connections",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Where coatings and other material industries overlap",
        "content": "Yashm's Persian-language website provides an example of a business combining industrial coatings, automotive refinish products, wood coatings and resins. Automotive refinish refers to coatings used in vehicle repair and repainting. The company lists primers, intermediate coats, two-component topcoats and putties, illustrating the different products within a repair-coating system. [s4]\n\nIts resin descriptions also identify applications involving pipes and composite manufacturing. Simab's categories extend further, including textiles, paper, printing and packaging, leather and pressure-sensitive adhesives. The useful distinction is between a resin's chemical family and the application for which a particular grade is supplied: a broad family name alone does not establish that products are interchangeable. [s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpreting-industry-claims",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What catalogues establish—and what remains open",
        "content": "The inspected catalogues establish a documented range of Iranian commercial offerings. They also show specialization: Isatis lists paint driers and additives alongside resins, while Simab emphasizes water-based materials and several downstream markets. This is evidence of product positioning, rather than proof of market leadership, independently verified performance or the scale of domestic substitution for imports. [s2][s3]\n\nA stronger contemporary assessment would connect these product descriptions with inspected production records, input sourcing and independent technical evidence. Company expansion histories cannot substitute for annual output, and individual portfolios cannot establish national demand. The supported conclusion is a diverse manufacturing field whose current scale and performance still require further research. [s1][s2][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
